<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Proserpina Dam</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persephone">Proserpina</a> Dam is a Roman gravity dam near Mérida in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, built probably in the 1st or 2nd century CE to supply <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emerita-augusta">Emerita Augusta</a>, capital of Lusitania. An earth embankment faced with a masonry wall some 425 metres long holds back a reservoir that fed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct-of-los-milagros">Aqueduct of Los Milagros</a>. Still in use, it is one of the largest Roman dams to survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/proserpina-dam.jpg" alt="Proserpina Dam" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alonso de Mendoza · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Embalse_de_Proserpina,_M%C3%A9rida_(2015).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
